/**
\addtogroup optimizers_second_order

This page documents two second-order optimization methods in namespace `laplace::optimizers`: Newton's method in `<laplace/optimizers/newton.hpp>` and a trust-region method with the exact dense subproblem in `<laplace/optimizers/trust_region.hpp>`. Both are header-only templates that run on the host only; nothing is marked `LAPLACE_HD`. Because they call `laplace::dense` through \ref laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_solver "dense_curvature_solver" and the symmetric eigendecomposition, a program that uses them links `Laplace::dense`. A run is `minimize(newton_optimizer<>{}, f, x0)`, using the objectives, stopping rules, line searches and report documented on \ref optimizers "the optimizers page". With the default decrement tolerance \f$10^{-6}\f$, both methods converge on the standard test problems and on quadratics with condition number up to \f$10^{12}\f$, with \f$f - f^*\f$ at most \f$6.0 \times 10^{-7}\f$ (Powell singular) in the runs measured below.

## Newton's method

Newton's method minimizes a \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_objective "curvature_objective" on the host, using \ref laplace::optimizers::decrement_stop_rule "decrement_stop_rule" and \ref laplace::optimizers::armijo_line_search "armijo_line_search" (Dennis and Schnabel, 1996, Algorithm A6.3.1) by default. When the curvature matrix has a Cholesky factor, it takes the Newton step; otherwise it replaces each eigenvalue by its absolute value, raised to a relative floor (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, section 3.4), and adds a negative-curvature step along the lowest eigenvector when needed (Moré and Sorensen, 1979). Step bounds limit both the modified direction and the final step (Dennis and Schnabel, 1996), and the line search backtracks from unit step, switching to a second-order decrease test along negative curvature at a saddle (Moré and Sorensen, 1979). Without a Cholesky factor, the decrement is quiet NaN so that no indefinite or singular point converges by the decrement.

An objective that already factorizes its curvature matrix can avoid a second factorization per step by modeling \ref laplace::optimizers::factorized_curvature_objective "factorized_curvature_objective", which refines `curvature_objective` with `factorized_curvature()` returning a pointer to `curvature_solver_for<curvature_type>::type` (or `nullptr`). When `factorized_curvature()` returns a non-null pointer holding the positive-definite factor of the exact `H` just written by `value_gradient_curvature()`, `newton_optimizer::run()` reads its `last_factorization()` and solves for the Newton step and decrement with that factor instead of factorizing `H` again; when it returns `nullptr` or the concept is not modeled, Newton factorizes `H` itself. Because the handed factor belongs to the same matrix `H`, the Newton steps and iterates do not change (checked in `tests/engine/test_inner_fit.cpp` on `engine::row_model_objective`, giving identical statuses, iteration counts, and evaluation counts, zero point difference on gcc and a bound of \f$10^{-10}\f$ relative on the point). See \ref laplace::optimizers::newton_optimizer "newton_optimizer" for the formulas, settings, statuses, and cost.

## The trust region

The trust-region method minimizes a \ref laplace::optimizers::curvature_objective "curvature_objective" whose curvature type is \ref laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_matrix "dense_curvature_matrix", using \ref laplace::optimizers::decrement_stop_rule "decrement_stop_rule" by default. Each trial step minimizes the quadratic model over a Euclidean ball of the current radius (Moré and Sorensen, 1983): it takes the Newton step when positive definite and inside the ball, and otherwise solves the secular equation in the eigenbasis, including the hard case along the lowest eigenvector. Trial steps are accepted or rejected and the radius is shrunk or expanded from the ratio of actual to predicted decrease (Nocedal and Wright, 2006, Algorithm 4.1), with non-finite trial values shrinking the radius. As in Newton's method, the decrement is quiet NaN wherever the curvature has no Cholesky factor, and the stopping rule is asked only at accepted points. See \ref laplace::optimizers::trust_region_optimizer "trust_region_optimizer" for the formulas, settings, statuses, and cost.

## Choosing between them

On quadratics of condition number up to \f$10^{12}\f$, Newton's method takes one iteration because the full Newton step reaches the minimizer at once, whereas the trust region takes 5 trials from the default radius \f$1\f$ while expanding to reach it. On Brown badly scaled, where the Hessian turns indefinite after the first step, the trust region adapts its radius and converges in 31 trials, whereas Newton's method takes 184 iterations under its default cap on the modified direction. Both methods leave a saddle point and reach a local minimum even when started with zero gradient.

## Accuracy and cost

The figures below were measured with gcc on one thread and the default decrement tolerance \f$10^{-6}\f$. Every run in the table converges, and adding \f$10^6\f$ to \f$f\f$ gives the same counts.

| Problem | Newton iterations | Newton value evaluations | Trust-region iterations | Trust-region value evaluations |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Rosenbrock, \f$n = 2\f$ | 23 | 55 | 24 | 46 |
| extended Rosenbrock, \f$n = 100\f$ | 23 | 55 | 23 | 45 |
| Powell singular | 12 | 25 | 12 | 25 |
| Beale | 6 | 15 | 6 | 12 |
| Brown badly scaled | 184 | 375 | 31 | 63 |
| helical valley | 14 | 31 | 8 | 16 |
| Wood | 41 | 102 | 44 | 83 |
| quadratic, \f$n = 10\f$, condition number \f$\kappa = 10^2\f$ | 1 | 3 | 5 | 11 |
| quadratic, \f$\kappa = 10^6\f$ | 1 | 3 | 5 | 11 |
| quadratic, \f$\kappa = 10^{12}\f$ | 1 | 3 | 5 | 11 |
| \f$x_1^2 + x_2^4 / 4 - x_2^2 / 2\f$ from \f$(1, 0)\f$ | 1 | 3 | 5 | 11 |
| Rosenbrock from \f$(0, 1)\f$ | 2 | 5 | 4 | 9 |

On extended Rosenbrock with \f$n = 100\f$, a run takes 1529 microseconds for Newton and 1966 microseconds for the trust region; the other problems take between 5 and 155 microseconds per run.

The tests check that the standard problems with constants \f$0\f$, \f$1\f$, \f$10^3\f$, and \f$10^6\f$ added to \f$f\f$, and quadratics with condition number \f$\kappa\f$ from \f$10^2\f$ to \f$10^{12}\f$, end converged with \f$f - f^* \le 2 \times 10^{-6} + 8 \varepsilon |f^*|\f$ and a positive definite final factorization; on the quadratics, they also check \f$|x_i - x^*_i| \le \sqrt{4 \times 10^{-6}} + 10^{-9} \kappa\f$ and that Newton takes at most 2 iterations. Both methods reach a minimum \f$(0, \pm 1)\f$ of \f$x_1^2 + x_2^4 / 4 - x_2^2 / 2\f$ from \f$(1, 0)\f$, where \f$H\f$ is indefinite and \f$g_2 = 0\f$, and from the saddle \f$(0, 0)\f$ itself, and both converge on Rosenbrock from \f$(0, 1)\f$. A non-finite trial value is refused (\f$x - \log x\f$ from \f$10\f$), and a non-finite start gives `start_not_admissible`. None of these ends with a success: a noisy objective, \f$\sum_i \cosh x_i\f$ from \f$700\f$ with 100 iterations (`iteration_budget`), \f$\sum_i |x_i|\f$ (decrement NaN throughout), and a change rule (`stopped_on_change`). For details of the measurements, see `docs/numerics.md`.

## Example

The example minimizes the Rosenbrock function \f$f(x) = 100 (x_2 - x_1^2)^2 + (1 - x_1)^2\f$ from \f$x_0 = (-1.2, 1)\f$ with `trust_region_optimizer`, after `Kokkos::initialize`.

```cpp
#include <laplace/optimizers/minimize.hpp>
#include <laplace/optimizers/trust_region.hpp>

struct rosenbrock_curvature_objective {
  using curvature_type = laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_matrix;

  double value(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x) const {
    const double t1 = x(1) - x(0) * x(0);
    const double t2 = 1.0 - x(0);
    return 100.0 * t1 * t1 + t2 * t2;
  }

  double value_gradient(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x,
                        const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector& g) const {
    const double t1 = x(1) - x(0) * x(0);
    const double t2 = 1.0 - x(0);
    g(0) = -400.0 * x(0) * t1 - 2.0 * t2;
    g(1) = 200.0 * t1;
    return 100.0 * t1 * t1 + t2 * t2;
  }

  double value_gradient_curvature(const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_point& x,
                                  const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector& g,
                                  laplace::optimizers::dense_curvature_matrix& H) const {
    H(0, 0) = 1200.0 * x(0) * x(0) - 400.0 * x(1) + 2.0;
    H(0, 1) = -400.0 * x(0);
    H(1, 0) = H(0, 1);
    H(1, 1) = 200.0;
    return value_gradient(x, g);
  }
};

bool rosenbrock_trust_region_example() {
  laplace::optimizers::optimizer_vector x0("x0", 2);
  x0(0) = -1.2;
  x0(1) = 1.0;
  rosenbrock_curvature_objective f;
  laplace::optimizers::trust_region_optimizer<> opt;
  const laplace::optimizers::optimizer_report report = laplace::optimizers::minimize(opt, f, x0);
  return laplace::optimizers::optimizer_succeeded(report.status);
}
```

## Module types

In namespace `laplace::optimizers`:
- \ref laplace::optimizers::factorized_curvature_objective "factorized_curvature_objective"
- \ref laplace::optimizers::newton_optimizer "newton_optimizer"
- \ref laplace::optimizers::trust_region_optimizer "trust_region_optimizer"

## References

- J. E. Dennis and R. B. Schnabel (1996), Numerical Methods for Unconstrained Optimization and Nonlinear Equations, Society for Industrial and Applied Mathematics. https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611971200
- J. Nocedal and S. J. Wright (2006), Numerical Optimization, 2nd edition, Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-0-387-40065-5
- J. J. Moré and D. C. Sorensen (1983), Computing a trust region step, SIAM Journal on Scientific and Statistical Computing, 4(3), 553–572. https://doi.org/10.1137/0904038
- J. J. Moré and D. C. Sorensen (1979), On the use of directions of negative curvature in a modified Newton method, Mathematical Programming, 16(1), 1–20. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01582091

*/
